# Topic sources, study links & authentic images (always-on)

Applies to **every** 365-calendar / daily neuro-news / council digest topic on drwasifmalik.com.

## Required content blocks
1. **Primary study/source** with a real link (PubMed, PMC, DOI, journal, or reputable clinical news as appropriate). Name the study/authors/year when known.
2. **Further reading / Study & management:** **3–5** curated links for patients and doctors — mix of peer-reviewed (PubMed/PMC/guidelines), stable textbook/chapter URLs when available, and reputable reviews/news when relevant. Prefer open-access.
3. **Images:** Prefer **authentic clinical/radiological figures from the cited open sources** (e.g. PMC OA figures under CC-BY / CC-BY-NC) over generic Imagine clinic tropes when a suitable licensed figure exists. Always attribute: source name + link + license note. Download + upload to WP media (do not hotlink fragile CDNs long-term).
4. If no suitable OA figure is found: use content-faithful Imagine **and still** provide real citations + further reading. Never invent DOIs/PMIDs/figure licenses.

## Pattern
Example pattern (topic-matched OA surgical/radiology paper):  
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6417310/ — cite the study, link the article, attribute any reused figure (journal/authors via PMC + license).

## Monolingual i18n
- Section labels translated per EN / UR / AR; display only active `?lang=`.
- URLs stay Latin/ASCII. Contact phones stay Western Latin digits.
- Never bilingual stacked “Primary source · اصل ماخذ” in one HTML body.

## Pipeline owners
- Council: `daily_neuro_news.py` (citation + further-reading HTML), `image_policy.py` (PMC/OA figure prefer → Imagine fallback).
- Homepage MU / droplet publishers must emit the same blocks monolingually into `post_content` + `_dwf_nn_body_ur` / `_dwf_nn_body_ar`.
- **`MAX_UPLOADS_PER_DAY = 1`** unchanged — never a second calendar day the same PKT day.

## Do not
- Fabricate citations, PMIDs, DOIs, or licenses.
- Scrape paywalled images.
- Skip further reading when publishing a calendar brief.
- Put doctor-face photos as featured images.
